Adapted code
------------

figwig (https://github.com/jmschrei/figwig), MIT license, Copyright (c) 2026 Jacob Schreiber
  - src/BamReader.{h,cpp}: the BGZF / BAM reading approach (find the block boundaries, inflate
    the blocks independently, decode the records) follows figwig/bam2bw.py.
  - src/BigWigWriter.{h,cpp}: figwig/writer.py served as a reference for the bigWig layout.
  - src/FigWig.{h,cpp}, src/GenomeTrackImportFigwig.cpp, R/track-figwig.R: written from figwig's format specification (docs/format.rst).
  - tests/testthat/fixtures/figwig/make_fixtures.py: follows figwig's tests/format_fixtures.py; the .fw test files
    next to it were written by figwig.

memesuite-lite (https://github.com/jmschrei/memesuite-lite), MIT license, Copyright (c) 2025 Jacob Schreiber
  - src/PwmFastScan.h: the thresholded PWM scan (5-mer codes, block weight tables, upper
    bounds that skip windows) is adapted from memelite/fimo.py (_qmer_codes, _block_layout,
    _qmer_tables, _fast_hits).

tangermeme (https://github.com/jmschrei/tangermeme), MIT license, Copyright (c) 2024-2026 Jacob Schreiber
  - tests/testthat/test-gseq-shuffle.R: the negative control reimplements tangermeme's dinucleotide shuffle
    (tangermeme/ersatz.py), to show it is not a uniform draw.
  - R/extract-windows.R: the window options (input and output widths, jitter margin, exclusion,
    count filters) follow the design of tangermeme/io.py extract_loci; no code is included.

Algorithms and formats used without code from their sources
-----------------------------------------------------------

  - k-mer preserving shuffle (src/SeqShuffle.{h,cpp}): implemented from Altschul & Erickson
    (1985), Kandel et al. (1996) and Jiang et al. (2008, uShuffle).
  - HOMER consensus (R/motif-export.R): HOMER's rule (the IUPAC code of every base within 0.15
    of the most probable one). HOMER is GPL-licensed; only the rule is used, no HOMER code.
  - bigWig writer layout (src/BigWigWriter.{h,cpp}): block and index sizes and the choice of
    zoom levels follow libBigWig (https://github.com/dpryan79/libBigWig, MIT, Devon Ryan).
  - bigWig, BAM and MEME / JASPAR / HOMER motif formats: from their published specifications.

